{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfyjukudnqTSPhy1McSdc","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I'd go with **`maxRetryCount`**, because it's harder to misread:\n\n- **`max`** tells you right away that it's an upper bound.\n- **`Count`** tells you it's a number of retries. `retryLimit` could also mean a time limit or a rate limit, and that ambiguity can matter in code that also deals with timeouts.\n\n`retryLimit` is shorter and reads well in a check like `if (attempts >= retryLimit)`, so it's a fine choice if nothing nearby involves time.\n\nTwo other things to consider:\n- **`maxRetries`** is shorter and just as clear, and it's a common convention (axios-retry, AWS SDKs, and many HTTP clients use it).\n- Whatever name you pick, make clear whether it counts **retries** or **total attempts**. A value of 3 could mean 3 or 4 calls in total, which causes off-by-one bugs. If it counts attempts, `maxAttempts` says so.\n\nIf your codebase already uses a pattern like `maxX` or `xLimit`, match it. Consistency with the code around it matters more than which of these you choose."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11530,"cache_read_input_tokens":25644,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11530},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ff01f16c-7b3f-5ea5-8051-7a95ac7cd1d1","uuid":"5b69af07-3c50-49fc-a1ec-a174373f2ddf","timestamp":"2026-10-13T06:11:31.221Z","request_id":"req_011CfyjukiiY7fPtgwXN7awS"}
